.TH CHECKMATE 1 "@DATE@" "checkmate @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
checkmate \- command-based compliance checks for Linux, macOS and Windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B checkmate run
.RI [ options ]
.I policy.yml
.br
.B checkmate validate
.RI [ options ]
.I policy.yml
.br
.B checkmate list
.RI [ options ]
.I policy.yml
.br
.B checkmate export
.RI [ options ]
.IR report.json ...
.br
.B checkmate diff
.RI [ options ]
.I old new
.br
.B checkmate verify
.RI [ options ]
.I policy.yml
.br
.B checkmate sign \-\-key
.I name.key file ...
.br
.B checkmate keygen
.I name
.br
.B checkmate facts
.br
.B checkmate version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B checkmate
checks a host against a policy: a YAML file of checks, each of which runs a
command and compares its exit code and output with an expectation.
Policies are templated with Jinja2, can select commands per platform, and
include other policy files.
Results are printed as text together with a weighted compliance score, and
exported as JSON, JSON Lines, CSV, HTML, PDF, JUnit XML, XCCDF, ARF,
Prometheus metrics, ATT&CK Navigator layers or syslog messages.
.PP
checkmate ships policies for the CIS Benchmarks (Linux distributions,
Windows, macOS, PostgreSQL, Apache httpd), DISA STIGs, BSI IT-Grundschutz,
Si001 of the Swiss Federal Administration and MITRE ATT&CK, plus examples
for systems, files, networks, hardware and applications.
The format of policy files is described in
.BR checkmate-policy (5).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Run the selected checks and report the results.
The exit status tells whether the host is compliant.
.TP
.B validate
Load and expand a policy and report errors without running any check.
.TP
.B list
List the selected checks; with
.B \-v
also the command each check would run on this host.
.TP
.B verify
List every file a policy is loaded from, with its SHA-256 hash, whether it
was shipped with checkmate, and who signed it.
Nothing is run.
The exit status is 0 if every file is shipped or signed by a trusted key.
.TP
.B sign
Write a signature file
.RI ( file .sig)
next to each given file, made with the private key given by
.BR \-\-key .
.TP
.B keygen
Write a new key pair:
.IR name .key
for
.B sign
and
.IR name .pub
for the file of trusted keys.
.TP
.B export
Convert JSON reports written by
.B run
into other formats.
Several reports (hosts or runs) are merged into one document.
A single format without
.B \-o
is written to standard output, otherwise every format to
.IR base . ext ;
.B \-
reads a report from standard input.
.TP
.B diff
Compare two JSON reports (new problems, fixed checks, newly waived checks, other status changes,
added and removed checks, score) or two policies (added, removed and
changed checks, field by field, after expanding loops, templates and
snippets).
Files ending in
.B .json
are reports,
.B .yml
and
.B .yaml
policies.
The exit status is 0 without and 1 with differences.
.TP
.B facts
Print the facts gathered for this host as JSON (operating system,
distribution, version, architecture, privileges, platform keys).
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be given before or after the policy file.
Single and double dashes are equivalent.
.SS Selecting checks (run, list)
.TP
.BI \-t ", " \-\-tags " expr"
Select checks by tags.
A comma means
.IR or ,
a plus sign means
.IR and :
.B compliance+full,quality
selects checks tagged both compliance and full, or tagged quality.
.TP
.BI \-\-skip\-tags " expr"
Exclude checks by tags, same syntax as
.BR \-\-tags .
.TP
.BI \-\-id " patterns"
Select checks by ID, comma-separated glob patterns, e.g.
.BR 'CIS-RHEL9-5.*,SSH-001' .
.SS Variables (run, validate, list)
.TP
.BI \-e ", " \-\-extra\-var " key=value"
Set a variable; repeatable.
Extra variables override the variables of the policy.
Lists, mappings, true/false and plain numbers are read as YAML, so
.B \-e ports='[22, 443]'
passes a list.
Every other value stays text exactly as typed:
.B \-e version=1.10
is not turned into 1.1 and
.B \-e mode=0644
not into 420.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-insecure\-policy
Load policy and waiver files as root even if another user could modify
them.
Without this option, checkmate running as root accepts only files that
are not group- or world-writable and are owned by root or by the owner of
the checkmate executable (the admin user with Homebrew).
.TP
.BI \-\-trusted\-keys " file"
File with the public keys that may sign policies, one per line;
repeatable.
Default:
.IR /etc/checkmate/trusted_keys .
.TP
.B \-\-require\-signed
Refuse policy, variable and waiver files that are neither shipped with
checkmate nor signed by a trusted key.
A file whose signature by a trusted key does not match its content is
refused in any case.
.SS Results (run)
.TP
.BI \-\-fail\-on " severity"
Lowest severity whose failures make the run non-compliant:
.BR info ", " low ", " medium ", " high " or " critical .
Failures below it are reported as warnings.
Default: the policy's
.BR defaults.fail_on ,
else
.BR low .
Errors always count.
.TP
.BI \-\-waivers " file"
Read accepted deviations from
.IR file ;
repeatable.
Waived checks that fail are reported as WAIVED with their reason and do not
make the run non-compliant.
Files named later take precedence.
See
.BR checkmate-policy (5).
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-waivers
Report every result as if no waiver existed.
.TP
.BI \-j ", " \-\-jobs " n"
Number of checks run in parallel.
Default: the number of CPUs.
.TP
.BI \-\-host " [user@]host[:port]"
Run the checks on another host over SSH instead of locally.
Nothing is installed there.
The facts are those of the host, so platform variants and
.B when
are decided for it.
checkmate uses the
.BR ssh (1)
program, with the configuration, keys and known hosts of the user; it
never asks for a password.
The environment variable
.B CHECKMATE_REQUIRE_SIGNED
Set to 1, it has the effect of
.BR \-\-require\-signed .
.TP
.B CHECKMATE_SSH
names another ssh program.
.TP
.B \-\-become
With
.BR \-\-host :
run every command through
.BR "sudo \-n" .
.TP
.BI \-i ", " \-\-inventory " file"
Run the checks on every host of an inventory file over SSH, as
.B \-\-host
does for one.
The file lists hosts and groups with their user, port, sudo setting and
variables; the variables
.B inventory_hostname
and
.B group_names
are set for every host.
The output is an overview of all hosts, and every export contains all of
them.
Windows hosts with OpenSSH are recognized and checked through PowerShell.
Hosts marked with
.B device
in the inventory, such as switches, get every command as it is written.
.TP
.BI \-l ", " \-\-limit " list"
With
.BR \-\-inventory :
only these hosts or groups, comma-separated.
Host names may contain * and ?.
.TP
.BI \-\-forks " n"
With
.BR \-\-inventory :
number of hosts checked at the same time.
Default: 5.
.SS Output (run, export, diff)
.TP
.BI \-\-format " format"
Output on standard output:
.B text
(default),
.BR json ,
.BR jsonl ,
.BR csv ,
.BR html ,
.BR junit ,
.BR xccdf ,
.BR arf ,
.B prom
or
.BR attack .
For
.BR diff :
.B text
or
.BR json .
.TP
.BI \-\-export " list"
Also write these formats to files, comma-separated, e.g.
.BR html,pdf,json .
Each file gets the extension of its format
.RB ( .html ", " .pdf ", " .junit.xml ", " .xccdf.xml ", " .arf.xml ", " .prom ", " .attack.json ", ...)."
.B syslog
sends every result to the local syslog daemon,
.BI syslog: url
to a collector:
.BR udp:// ,
.B tcp://
or
.BI tls:// host\fR[\fB: port\fR]
with an optional
.BI ?facility= name\fR.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " base"
Base name or directory of the exported files.
Default for
.BR run :
.IR checkmate\-<host>\-<time> ;
for
.BR export :
the name of the report.
Files are replaced atomically and created with mode 0640.
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-format " list"
.RB ( export )
Formats to write, comma-separated, including
.B pdf
and
.BR syslog .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show the severity and the reason of every result on one line.
Repeatable:
.B \-vv
adds commands, output, rationale and remediation of failed checks,
.B \-vvv
(or
.BR \-\-debug )
adds debug details of every check, the facts and the loaded files.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Show only failed checks and the summary.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " when"
Colored output:
.B auto
(default, on a terminal),
.B always
or
.BR never .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Same as
.BR "\-\-color never" .
.SH RESULTS
.TP
.B PASS
The expectation holds.
.TP
.B FAIL
The expectation does not hold and the severity is at or above the
threshold.
.TP
.B WARN
The expectation does not hold, but the severity is below the threshold
(or
.BR info ).
.TP
.B ERROR
The check could not be evaluated: timeout, command not found, unexpected
exit code, invalid definition.
Errors always count as non-compliant.
.TP
.B WAIVED
The check failed, warned or errored, but a waiver accepts it.
.TP
.B SKIP
Excluded by
.B when
or
.BR only_if .
.TP
.B N/A
No command variant for this platform.
.SH INVENTORY
The file given with
.B \-\-inventory
is YAML with three optional keys.
.B defaults
holds settings for every host,
.B groups
maps group names to groups, and
.B hosts
lists hosts outside of any group.
A host is a name, or a mapping with
.B name
and settings; a group has settings and its own
.BR hosts .
.PP
The settings are
.B user
and
.B port
for the connection,
.B address
(hosts only) if it differs from the name,
.B become
to run commands through
.BR "sudo \-n" ,
.B device
with a platform name such as ios or junos for a host without a shell, and
.B vars
with variables for the policy.
Settings and variables go from general to specific: defaults, the groups
of a host in file order, the host.
Inventory variables override those of the policy;
.B \-e
overrides both.
.PP
.RS
.nf
defaults: { user: audit, become: true }
groups:
  web:
    vars: { role: web }
    hosts:
      \- web01.example.net
      \- { name: web02, address: 10.0.0.12, port: 2222 }
  switches:
    device: ios
    become: false
    hosts: [sw\-core\-01.example.net]
hosts:
  \- backup01.example.net
.fi
.RE
.PP
A host that does not answer or cannot be checked has no results.
It is listed with the reason and counts neither as compliant nor as
non-compliant.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Compliant: no FAIL and no ERROR.
.TP
.B 1
Not compliant: at least one FAIL or ERROR.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, invalid policy or waiver file, no checks selected, or no check
evaluated (all skipped or not applicable).
.TP
.B 3
With
.BR \-\-inventory :
no host is non-compliant, but at least one could not be checked, for
example because it is unreachable.
Nothing is known about such a host.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colored output when set.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Width used to shorten result lines when standard output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B CHECKMATE_REQUIRE_SIGNED
Set to 1, it has the effect of
.BR \-\-require\-signed .
.TP
.B CHECKMATE_SSH
The ssh program used for
.B \-\-host
and
.BR \-\-inventory ,
instead of
.BR ssh .
.PP
Commands run with the environment of checkmate plus the policy's
.B env
settings.
On POSIX systems they run with
.B LC_ALL=C
under
.B bash \-o pipefail
(or
.BR sh ),
on Windows under PowerShell with
.BR "$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/checkmate/cis/
CIS Benchmark policies, one per platform.
.TP
.I /usr/share/checkmate/stig/
DISA STIG policies.
.TP
.I /usr/share/checkmate/examples/
Example policies, including the BSI IT-Grundschutz, Si001 and MITRE ATT&CK
policies under
.I compliance/
and
.IR attack/ .
.PP
With Homebrew the policies are installed below
.IR "$(brew \-\-prefix)/share/checkmate/" .
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the CIS Level 1 server profile on a RHEL 9 host:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo checkmate run /usr/share/checkmate/cis/rhel9.yml \-\-tags cis\-level1\-server
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a policy on another host over SSH, with sudo, and on the web servers
of an inventory with one report for all of them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
checkmate run baseline.yml \-\-host audit@web01.example.net \-\-become
checkmate run baseline.yml \-i hosts.yml \-l web \-\-export html,pdf \-o reports/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show only failures of severity high and above, with details:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo checkmate run baseline.yml \-\-fail\-on high \-q \-vv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Write JSON, JUnit and HTML reports for a CI pipeline
.RI ( report.json ", " report.junit.xml ", " report.html ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
checkmate run baseline.yml \-\-export json,junit,html \-o report
.fi
.RE
.PP
Update Prometheus metrics and send the results to a SIEM:
.PP
.RS
.nf
checkmate run baseline.yml \-q \-\-export prom,syslog:tls://siem.example.com \-o /var/lib/checkmate/latest
.fi
.RE
.PP
Merge the reports of all hosts into one HTML and PDF report:
.PP
.RS
.nf
checkmate export \-f html,pdf \-o fleet reports/*.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show what changed between two runs, or between two versions of a policy:
.PP
.RS
.nf
checkmate diff monday.json tuesday.json
checkmate diff baseline\-1.4.yml baseline\-1.5.yml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Accept documented deviations, and check the raw state for an audit:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo checkmate run /usr/share/checkmate/cis/debian12.yml \-\-waivers /etc/checkmate/waivers.yml
sudo checkmate run /usr/share/checkmate/cis/debian12.yml \-\-ignore\-waivers
.fi
.RE
.PP
Look for traces of ATT&CK techniques and open the result in the ATT&CK
Navigator:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo checkmate run /usr/share/checkmate/examples/attack/linux.yml \-\-export attack \-o web01
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show which commands a policy would run on this host:
.PP
.RS
.nf
checkmate list \-v /usr/share/checkmate/examples/system/ssh.yml
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR checkmate-policy (5)
.PP
Documentation: https://rtulke.github.io/checkmate/
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at https://github.com/rtulke/checkmate/issues.
